The statement is **TRUE**. Here is the proof.

## Proof

**Key observation.** For any $A \in G$, the map
$$L_A : M \longrightarrow M, \qquad x \longmapsto Ax$$
is a diffeomorphism of $M$ onto itself.

*Why $L_A$ maps $M$ to $M$:* A point of $M$ has the form $Bx_0$ for some $B \in G$. Then $L_A(Bx_0) = (AB)x_0$, and since $G$ is a subgroup, $AB \in G$, so $(AB)x_0 \in M$.

*Why $L_A$ is a diffeomorphism:* It is the restriction to $M$ of the linear map $A:\mathbb{R}^n\to\mathbb{R}^n$, hence smooth. Its inverse is $L_{A^{-1}}$ (since $A^{-1}\in G$), which is likewise smooth. So $L_A$ is a smooth diffeomorphism of $M$.

**Differential of $L_A$.** Since $L_A$ is the restriction of a *linear* map on the ambient $\mathbb{R}^n$, its differential at any point $x_0\in M$ is simply the linear map itself:
$$d(L_A)_{x_0} : T_{x_0}M \longrightarrow T_{Ax_0}M, \qquad v \longmapsto Av.$$
Being the differential of a diffeomorphism, this is a linear isomorphism between the two tangent spaces. Therefore
$$A(T_{x_0}M) = d(L_A)_{x_0}\bigl(T_{x_0}M\bigr) = T_{Ax_0}M.$$

**Explicit verification via the Lie algebra (optional cross-check).** Let $\mathfrak{g}=\operatorname{Lie}(G)\subset\mathfrak{gl}(n)$. The orbit map $\phi:G\to M$, $B\mapsto Bx_0$, gives
$$T_{x_0}M = \mathfrak{g}\cdot x_0 = \{Xx_0 : X\in\mathfrak{g}\}, \qquad T_{Ax_0}M = \mathfrak{g}\cdot(Ax_0)=\{YAx_0:Y\in\mathfrak{g}\}.$$
For $A\in G$, conjugation $C_A(B)=ABA^{-1}$ is a Lie-group automorphism of $G$, so its differential $\operatorname{Ad}(A):\mathfrak{g}\to\mathfrak{g}$, $X\mapsto AXA^{-1}$, is a Lie-algebra automorphism; in particular $A\mathfrak{g}A^{-1}=\mathfrak{g}$, equivalently $A^{-1}\mathfrak{g}A=\mathfrak{g}$. Writing $YAx_0 = A(A^{-1}YA)x_0$ and noting that $Z:=A^{-1}YA$ ranges over all of $\mathfrak{g}$ as $Y$ does,
$$T_{Ax_0}M = \{AZx_0 : Z\in\mathfrak{g}\} = A\{Zx_0:Z\in\mathfrak{g}\} = A(\mathfrak{g}\cdot x_0)=A(T_{x_0}M).$$

Both arguments confirm the equality. $\blacksquare$

